<title>BELGIUM: Farm ministers discuss export refund/animal welfare link.</title>
<headline>Farm ministers discuss export refund/animal welfare link.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-07-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Farm ministers discussed technical problems with a proposal to make payment of export refunds for farm animals dependent on respect of animal welfare rules, Luxembourg Farm Minister Fernand Boden said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;There's concern about public opinion following distressing scenes on television of animals being mistreated,&quot; Boden said after a meeting of EU farm ministers.</p>
<p>Among the problems were the monitoring of the welfare of animals after they had been transported outside the EU.</p>
<p>Seven countries, including Germany, Sweden and Britain, want speedy action to improve animal welfare. But some exporting countries, such as France and Ireland, are cautious about the cost of stricter animal welfare rules.</p>
<p>A decision cannot be taken until after the European Parliament has given its opinion in October.</p>
<p>The EU exports about 600,000 cattle, sheep and goats annually -- mostly to Egypt, Libya, Lebanon and Turkey. But EU livestock exports are mostly in the form of meat.</p>
</text>
